{"name":"Anemone White","hex":"#f9efe4","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"anemone","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any plant of the genus Anemone, of the Ranunculaceae (or buttercup) family, such as the windflower.","A sea anemone."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/anemone"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Anemone","blocks":[{"text":"Anemone is a genus of flowering plants in the buttercup family Ranunculaceae. Plants of the genus are commonly called windflowers. They are native to the temperate and subtropical regions of all regions except Australia, New Zealand, and Antarctica. The genus is closely related to several other genera including Anemonoides, Anemonastrum, Hepatica, and Pulsatilla. Some botanists include these genera within Anemone."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Anemones5.jpg/330px-Anemones5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anemone"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Anemone_%28PSF%29.png/500px-Anemone_%28PSF%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Anemone_%28PSF%29.png/500px-Anemone_%28PSF%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"An illustration of an anemone","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnemone_(PSF).png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Anemone_drummondii.jpg/500px-Anemone_drummondii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Anemone_drummondii.jpg/960px-Anemone_drummondii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Anemone_drummondii.jpg/500px-Anemone_drummondii.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AAnemone_drummondii.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Ranuncolaceae_-_Anemone_hortensis-2.JPG/500px-Ranuncolaceae_-_Anemone_hortensis-2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Ranuncolaceae_-_Anemone_hortensis-2.JPG/960px-Ranuncolaceae_-_Anemone_hortensis-2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Ranuncolaceae_-_Anemone_hortensis-2.JPG/500px-Ranuncolaceae_-_Anemone_hortensis-2.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnai